Attach to a terminal somebody else already owns, instead of forking one.

fbterm always calls forkpty() and execs a shell on the fresh slave, which
makes fbterm the parent of the login program. A child inherits its parent's
identity, so on Samsara the getty it execs is a uid 1000 program, and a getty
that cannot setuid(0) refuses every root login -- after already having
accepted the password, so the screen says only "could not set up the
session".

That is not a getty bug: a getty has to start privileged because it becomes
whoever logs in, and nothing unprivileged can start one. So the kernel starts
it and passes the terminal to fbterm with --attach-fd=N. fbterm reads and
writes that descriptor and starts no process of its own, which is the
arrangement a terminal emulator is normally given when told to use a terminal
somebody else opened.

Without the option this is exactly upstream.

Generated with `git diff` against the pinned fbterm commit
419c6be55413a7069fbcc66883d58caa94ba3933. Regenerate with
ports/fbterm/build.sh; never edit by hand.

